The issue was caused by the `Highlander` custom `argparse.Action` checking if the current value in the namespace was equal to the default value before deciding whether to raise an error. This meant that if the option was provided multiple times but the first time it used the default value, no error was raised.

I fixed this by introducing a tracking mechanism within the `argparse.Namespace` object (using a private attribute `_highlander_seen`) to keep track of which options using the `Highlander` action have already been encountered, regardless of their value.

```python
class Highlander(argparse.Action):
    """make sure some option is only given once"""

    def __call__(self, parser, namespace, values, option_string=None):
        if hasattr(namespace, "_highlander_seen") and namespace._highlander_seen.get(self.dest, False):
            raise argparse.ArgumentError(self, "There can be only one.")
        if not hasattr(namespace, "_highlander_seen"):
            namespace._highlander_seen = {}
        namespace._highlander_seen[self.dest] = True
        setattr(namespace, self.dest, values)
```

