The issue was caused by a bug in the `get_emp_bank_ifsc_code` function in `hrms/payroll/report/bank_remittance/bank_remittance.py`. Inside the loop iterating over `salary_slips`, the code was using the loop variable `code` from the preceding loop (which contained the last employee's information) instead of the current `slip.employee` to look up the IFSC code. This resulted in all employees in the report being assigned the IFSC code of the last employee processed in the `ifsc_codes` list.

I fixed this by correctly using `slip.employee` to access the `ifsc_codes_map`, and added a check to ensure the employee exists in the map before attempting access.

```python
def get_emp_bank_ifsc_code(salary_slips):
	emp_names = [d.employee for d in salary_slips]
	ifsc_codes = get_all("Employee", [("name", "IN", emp_names)], ["ifsc_code", "name"])

	ifsc_codes_map = {}
	for code in ifsc_codes:
		ifsc_codes_map[code.name] = code

	for slip in salary_slips:
		if slip.employee in ifsc_codes_map:
			slip["ifsc_code"] = ifsc_codes_map[slip.employee]["ifsc_code"]

	return salary_slips
```

